Q: How to change the camera field of View in unity via script A: <p>You can access the main camera field of view like this  <code>Camera.main.fieldOfView</code> and then use  <code>Mathf.MoveTowards</code> to change the camera field of view over time instead of instantly, set the value that you want your field of view to change to    in <code>DestFov</code></p>

<pre><code>public float speed = 5.0;
public float DestFov=50.0;

 void Start () {
     fov = Camera.main.fieldOfView;
 }

 void Update () {
      Camera.main.fieldOfView = Mathf.MoveTowards(Camera.main.fieldOfView, DestFov, Time.deltaTime * speed);
 }
</code></pre>

Q: get HP Printer Serial Number via CMD A: <p>Quite late to the party, but...</p>
<p>This should work at least for HP Office Jet (tested with HP Office Jet 4500). I don't have access to any other models but I can imagine that it works for any network-managed (HP) printer.</p>
<pre><code>@echo off
setlocal
for /f %%a in ('curl &quot;http://192.168.0.000/index.htm?cat=info&amp;page=printerInfo#&quot; 2^&gt;nul ^|findstr /rc:&quot; *CN&quot;') do set &quot;SN=%%a&quot;
echo Serialnumber is %SN%.
</code></pre>
<p>This depends on the printer to be manufactured in China (Serial number starts with <code>CN...</code></p>
<p>The question explicitly asks for a <code>cmd</code> solution. With any language with proper HTML or REGEX support, there are surely better (safer) solutions (not depending on <code>CN...</code>).</p>
<p>Also, the <code>curl</code> command might be subject to improvement (first time I used it)</p>
<p>the relevant section in the HTML looks like:</p>
<pre><code>                  &lt;tr&gt;
                     &lt;td class=&quot;info&quot;&gt;

                        Product Serial Number
                     &lt;/td&gt;
                     &lt;td&gt;
                        CN000A000A00AA
                     &lt;/td&gt;
                  &lt;/tr&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I extracted the link to the info page from <code>curl &lt;printer-ip&gt;</code>, which gave me</p>
<pre><code>&lt;body onLoad=&quot;window.top.location.href='./index.htm?cat=info&amp;page=printerInfo'&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>(which surely can be used to get the correct page with different models)</p>
<p>(Note: <code>curl</code> is now available with WIN 10, so this probably won't have worked back in 2014 &quot;out of the box&quot;)</p>

Q: Have null / default value if column name does not exist in query result A: <p>If it is possible that some columns are missing from the query result, you can first select column indexes by name, and then check the index and select a value.<br />
This approach is also more effective for large data sizes.</p>
<pre><code>using (OleDbDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader())
 {
     int i_part_id=-1, i_name = -1, i_delivery_time = -1, i_price = -1;
     for(int i = 0; i &lt; reader.FieldCount; i++)
     {
         string colName=reader.GetName(i); 
         if (colName == &quot;part_id&quot;) i_part_id = i;
         if (colName == &quot;name&quot;) i_name = i;
         if (colName == &quot;delivery_time&quot;) i_delivery_time = i;
         if (colName == &quot;price&quot;) i_price = i;
      }
      while (reader.Read())
      {
         Part p = new Part();
         p.id =(reader[i_part_id] != DBNull.Value)? reader.GetString(i_part_id) : null;
         p.name = reader[i_name].ToString();
         p.deliveryTime = Convert.ToInt32(reader[i_delivery_time]);
         if (i_price &gt;= 0)
         {
             if (reader[i_price] != DBNull.Value) p.price = Convert.ToDecimal(reader[i_price]);
         }
         parts.Add(p);
      }
  }
</code></pre>
